     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "In nineteen fifty-two, Grace Hopper built the first compiler without writing code. Engineers manually plugged physical wires into giant patch panels to run equations. Critics claimed computers could only compute math, not read words. So Hopper created the A-Zero system on the UNIVAC. It automatically translated human words into machine binary. Suddenly, non-mathematicians could instruct complex computers. Then IBM released Fortran in nineteen fifty-seven based on her compiler. Coding shifted from physical wires into readable logic. Modern software relies on those automated translation layers. Every script runs on her foundation."
